    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks for explaining that. so if the LDL is high... does that mean it's like, actively building something up in my arteries? like how does the buildup actually happen over time?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "When LDL levels are high, these lipids can penetrate the artery walls and stick to damaged endothelial cells, forming fatty streaks. Over years, these accumulate into plaques that restrict blood flow and harden the artery walls, a process known as atherosclerosis. This buildup is often silent until it narrows the vessel enough to cause serious issues like a heart attack or stroke.",
      "source": "target",
