      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Yes, the process is often driven by an inflamed or \"stressed\" inner lining of your arteries, which acts like a magnet for the LDL particles. Small particles of LDL can then seep past the damaged wall into the chunk of cholesterol tissue beneath it and become trapped there. Over time, this trapped material triggers your immune system to send white blood cells and cholesterol crystals to clean up the mess, forming the unstable plaques you read about.",
